        "thinking": "Now I can see the pattern clearly. Both `heartbeat.ts` and `plugin-dispatch.ts` have the same `SIDECAR_SERVICE` → KV bridge fallback pattern. The plugins.ts routes handler also has the same pattern.\n\nFor F-08, I should extract a shared `sidecar-client.ts` module. The function signature should accept:\n- `SidecarServiceConfig`: optional DO namespace + optional direct URL + optional API key\n- `path`, `method`, `body`\n\nThen update all three callers.\n\nLet me create `src/worker/sidecar-client.ts` and update the three files.\n\n\nDefining the shared interface...\n\nI'm realizing that heartbeat.ts and plugin-dispatch.ts only have `SIDECAR_SERVICE` and KV available in their environment, not the direct URL or API key that the main handler has. So I need to think about how to handle this configuration difference when they call into the shared client.\n\nLooking at the actual usage patterns, routes/plugins.ts falls back through env vars (SIDECAR_SERVICE → SIDECAR_URL → 503), while the workflows use KV to look up the bridge URL (SIDECAR_SERVICE → KV SANDBOX_BRIDGE_URL → localhost:8788). These are fundamentally different fallback chains, which makes it tricky to unify them into a single function without the config object understanding which path to take.\n\nBut I realize the caller can handle the KV/env resolution upfront — the shared function just needs the resolved URL and API key passed in directly. For workflows, the KV read happens outside the step boundary anyway, so the caller fetches the bridge URL from KV first, then passes it to the shared function. This way the shared function stays simple and doesn't need to know about KV or env var lookups. , else → KV url fetch\n- `plugin-dispatch.ts` sidecarPost: if SIDECAR_SERVICE → DO fetch, else → bridge url fetch  \n- `plugins.ts` proxySidecar: if SIDECAR_SERVICE → DO fetch, else if SIDECAR_URL → direct fetch, else 503\n\nThe shared part is: \"given a DO namespace and/or a URL, make the right call\". The sidecar-client would accept:\n- `service?: DurableObjectNamespace | null` — the DO namespace  \n- `url: string` — the fallback URL (already resolved by caller)\n- `apiKey?: string | null` — the API key for the direct URL path\n\nThis would work for all three callers, as long as the caller pre-resolves the URL (from env var or KV).\n\nNow I'm implementing the sidecar-client to be minimal and focused—it just takes the already-resolved configuration and makes the appropriate fetch call based on what's available.",
